6a. Texture & Fiber: Lentils & Greens
Adding cooked lentils is an excellent idea.

Why: Legumes are the cornerstone of the Blue Zones diets. They provide the "second meal effect," stabilizing your blood sugar for hours after consumption.

How: Use Red Lentils. Unlike brown or green lentils which have skins that remain gritty when blended, split red lentils disintegrate into a creamy puree when cooked. They will thicken your shake without ruining the texture.

Greens: Since you are limiting oxalates (spinach), Kale or Collard Greens are your best bet.

Tip: If you have time, quickly blanching the kale (boiling for 30-60 seconds) removes the bitterness and makes it blend much smoother than raw kale, while preserving the calcium and vitamins.

6b. Nuts & Seeds: Walnuts & Brazil Nuts
Walnuts: Yes, switch to walnuts as your primary fat source. They are the only nut with significant ALA Omega-3s and have the highest antioxidant activity.

Brazil Nut: Absolutely keep 1 per day. It is the most reliable whole-food source of Selenium.

Note: Since you are blending 3 meals at once, toss 3 Brazil nuts into the morning blender batch to ensure the dosage is distributed.

7. The "Chew" Factor (Cephalic Phase Digestion)
Drinking 2,000 calories without chewing can lower satiety signaling and skip the first stage of digestion (release of enzymes in saliva).

The Carrot Solution: A large raw carrot is perfect. It cleans teeth and provides a satisfying crunch.

Other Options:

Raw Red Bell Pepper: High in Vitamin C (which helps iron absorption from your shake).

Apple: If you want something sweeter.

Cucumber slices: Hydrating and low calorie.

Snap Peas: Very quick, high fiber.

8. Japanese Sweet Potatoes
We can definitely include these. They are a staple in the Okinawan diet (one of the original Blue Zones).

Preparation: You must cook them first. Roast or steam a batch on Sunday.

Use: Peel them before blending. They add a creamy, custard-like texture and natural sweetness that pairs perfectly with vanilla.

Resistant Starch: If you cook them, cool them, and blend them cold (since you work from home and have a fridge), you increase the resistant starch, which feeds your gut microbiome.

9. Protein Powder Brands (EU Compliant/Safety)
Since you are concerned with heavy metals and want EU-standard strictness:

Top Recommendation: Vivo Life (Perform or Ritual).

Why: They are a UK-based company (EU compliant). They are one of the few brands that third-party test every batch for heavy metals and publish the results. They use fermented pea protein (easier digestion) and no artificial ingredients.

Vedge: Good reputation, USDA organic, but US-based.

Blueprint (Bryan Johnson): Likely very high quality given his protocol, but often expensive and includes a complex stack of other ingredients you might not want in a simple protein isolate.

Soy limit: If you keep your soy milk/tofu intake moderate, a pea/hemp blend protein powder is best to diversify your amino acid profile.

10. Nutrient Timing & Lifting
Lifting 4-6 PM vs. Diet: If you consume all your calories for breakfast and lunch (e.g., done eating by 2 PM), you might feel depleted by your 5 PM lift.

Recommendation: Save one of your 3 "bottles" for 3:30 PM (about 60-90 mins pre-workout). This ensures you have glycogen available for the lift.

Morning vs. Evening Lifting: If you want to switch to morning lifting, eating your calories after the lift (Breakfast/Lunch) is physiologically ideal for muscle repair. However, if you prefer afternoon lifting, just ensure you aren't going into it completely fasted if performance is the goal.

11. Algae Oil (Omega 3)
Since you want to take this as a capsule to avoid the "fishy" taste in your drink:

Brand Recommendation: Nordic Naturals Algae Omega.

Why: They are the gold standard for purity and freshness. They use 3rd party testing and are widely trusted by practitioners.

Dose: Aim for at least 300-500mg of combined EPA/DHA.

12. B12 Supplement
Brand Recommendation: Pure Encapsulations B12 (Adenosyl/Hydroxy) or Global Healing B12.

Why: Pure Encapsulations is "hypoallergenic" and strictly tested. Global Healing is a liquid liquid vegan formula that is very clean.

Form: Look for a blend of Methylcobalamin and Adenosylcobalamin (better than Cyanocobalamin).

13. Bananas & Oxidation
The Issue: You are correct. Bananas blended in the morning will turn brown and potentially taste metallic/fermented by 4 PM due to oxidation.

The Fix:

Leave them out: Use the Japanese Sweet Potato and maybe 1-2 Medjool Dates for sweetness instead. Dates do not oxidize like bananas.

Add Vitamin C: If you must use bananas, adding a squeeze of lemon juice or a dash of Amla powder (Vitamin C) into the blender slows oxidation.

Eat Separate: Eat the banana whole as a pre-workout snack.

The Revised "Perfect" Justin Protocol (Daily Batch)
This recipe hits your 2000 calorie goal, minimizes heavy metals, uses whole foods, and solves the texture/prep issues.

Instructions: Blend with water (approx 1.5 - 2 liters total liquid) in the morning. Pour into 3 large bottles/shakers. Keep in fridge.

The Base (Carbs & Fiber)

1.5 cups Rolled Oats: (Soak them overnight if you want smoother texture, or blend dry first into flour).

1 large Japanese Sweet Potato: Cooked, peeled, chilled.

1/2 cup Red Lentils: Dry measure, then boiled until mushy. (Prep a big batch on Sunday).

The Proteins

2 Scoops Vivo Life Protein (Vanilla): ~50g protein (Pea/Hemp blend).

1 cup Soy Milk (Organic): Adds creaminess, keeps soy protein low (<8g).

The Fats & Micros

1/4 cup Walnuts: Omega 3s.

2 tbsp Ground Flaxseed: Lignans and more Omega 3s.

3 Brazil Nuts: Selenium (remove if you eat them separate).

1 cup Kale or Collard Greens: Blanched or raw.

1 tsp Amla Powder: The highest antioxidant whole food (Dr. Greger favorite), also prevents oxidation of the drink.

1/2 tsp Turmeric + pinch Black Pepper: Anti-inflammatory.

5g Creatine Monohydrate: (Thorne or pure brand).

Flavor (No Artificials)

1 tsp Vanilla Extract: Pure.

2-3 Medjool Dates: Pitted (Adjust for sweetness preference).

Dash of Cinnamon: Blood sugar control.

The "Chew" (Consumed separately with meals)

Meal 1: 1 Large Carrot.

Meal 2: 1 Red Bell Pepper or Cucumber.

Meal 3: Handful of snap peas or an apple.

Supplements (Taken separately)

Morning: Vitamin D3 + K2, B12.

With Drink: Algae Oil Capsule (Fat aids absorption).

Summary of Stats (Approximate)
Calories: ~1900-2050 (Depending on potato/oat size)

Protein: ~110g (Complete profile)

Fat: Low-ish, mostly PUFA/MUFA from walnuts/flax. Zero trans fat.

Prep: 5 mins active time (if lentils/potatoes are prepped weekly).
